WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:15.503
This is the Korea Daily Brief from the CSIS Korea Chair, read by an AI voice. It's Tuesday, October 20. Three things today: North Korea threatens loudspeakers and strike assets at the border.

2
00:00:15.503 --> 00:00:28.263
President Trump casts doubt on a Kim summit at APEC. And South Korea's stock market takes its steepest single-day fall in recent weeks. That first story is the one to watch most closely.

3
00:00:28.263 --> 00:00:41.367
Kim Yo Jong has threatened to restart cross-border propaganda broadcasts and deploy additional strike assets along the Military Demarcation Line, and Seoul is warning of decisive retaliation.

4
00:00:41.367 --> 00:00:45.620
A missile launch anniversary falls in two days, on October 22.

5
00:00:46.820 --> 00:00:57.684
First, the border threat. Kim Yo Jong, the Deputy Director of the Workers' Party Department of Agitation and Propaganda, issued a statement through KCNA on October 19 and 20.

6
00:00:57.684 --> 00:01:09.672
Her words, as reported: "The situation along the southern border is grave. We are considering resuming loudspeaker broadcasts and deploying additional tactical strike assets along the border."

7
00:01:09.672 --> 00:01:19.662
South Korea's top military officer ordered stern responses. The Ministry of National Defense expressed, in its words, "grave concern," and pushed back directly.

8
00:01:19.662 --> 00:01:30.089
The ministry says South Korea's warning shots and broadcasts along the Military Demarcation Line are lawful defensive measures consistent with the armistice agreement.

9
00:01:30.089 --> 00:01:37.582
It warned that if North Korea uses these as a pretext for further provocation, it will be, quote, "resolutely punished."

10
00:01:37.582 --> 00:01:49.008
Marine Corps Commandant General Ju Il-suk told a parliamentary audit of the navy and marines that North Korea has made "various changes" to forces facing South Korea's border islands.

11
00:01:49.008 --> 00:02:01.558
Seoul, he said, has stepped up surveillance along the maritime border. The Unification Ministry is urging North Korea to halt what it calls armistice violations and engage with Seoul's dialogue offers.

12
00:02:01.558 --> 00:02:18.666
Unification Minister Chung Dong-young, in a separate meeting with a visiting German Bundestag member, said that resolving the Korea peninsula problem requires efforts to end U.S.-North Korea hostility and cooperation from the international community, proceeding in parallel.

13
00:02:18.666 --> 00:02:29.280
Keep in mind: the one-year anniversary of a North Korean short-range ballistic missile launch falls on October 22, two days from now, as these border tensions are rising.

14
00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:42.833
Second, the Trump-Kim summit question. President Trump told reporters at the White House on Monday that he was uncertain about prospects for a meeting with Kim Jong Un at China's APEC summit next month.

15
00:02:42.833 --> 00:02:55.369
That reverses earlier remarks that had raised expectations for an encounter. The shift follows a Chosun Ilbo report whose headline captured the gap precisely: "Chung Sees Summit in Sight, Trump Uncertain."

16
00:02:55.369 --> 00:03:07.050
The divergence suggests Unification Minister Chung Dong-young and the White House are not aligned on timeline. North Korea's state media has made no reciprocal signal of interest in a summit.

17
00:03:07.050 --> 00:03:20.320
That silence on Pyongyang's end matters. Without a public signal from Kim Jong Un's government, the question of whether any APEC encounter happens remains open, and, for now, the answer from Washington sounds like no.

18
00:03:21.520 --> 00:03:31.521
Third, South Korea's markets. South Korean AI data center builder Elice fell 32 percent on its first trading day on the Korea Stock Exchange.

19
00:03:31.521 --> 00:03:41.168
That plunge dragged chipmakers Samsung Electronics and SK Hynix lower and pushed Seoul's main stock index, the KOSPI, down 3.73 percent.

20
00:03:41.168 --> 00:03:48.686
Reuters called the selloff a reflection of, quote, "AI jitters," compounded by political unrest in France.

21
00:03:48.686 --> 00:03:58.120
Bloomberg reported the selloff erased an estimated 137 billion dollars from the Korea National Pension Service's chip-stock holdings.

22
00:03:58.120 --> 00:04:05.496
A former Bank of Korea governor warned separately that Korea's AI boom risks widening income inequality.

23
00:04:05.496 --> 00:04:19.895
Reuters framed Elice's debut as a, quote, "Korean canary in the AI coalmine," raising questions about whether global AI investment skepticism is reaching a turning point. The KOSPI closed at about 5,553.

24
00:04:19.895 --> 00:04:24.080
Elice down 32 percent on day one is the number to remember.

25
00:04:25.280 --> 00:04:38.528
Now to Pyongyang. KCNA led today with two stories. The first: Kim Jong Un attended a garment and shoe light industry expo in Pyongyang with his daughter, touring exhibits and touting regional policy achievements.

26
00:04:38.528 --> 00:04:49.402
That appearance was the state media's answer to viral social media rumors about Kim's health. KCNA released photos dated October 20 showing Kim with his daughter at the expo.

27
00:04:49.402 --> 00:04:56.213
The second top KCNA article covered Kim Yo Jong's border threat statement, which you heard in the first lead.

28
00:04:56.213 --> 00:05:05.462
State media framed it as a response to what Pyongyang characterizes as illegal South Korean military operations along the Military Demarcation Line.

29
00:05:05.462 --> 00:05:18.960
The bottom line from Pyongyang today: KCNA rebutted death rumors about Kim with his public appearance, amplified the border threat from his sister, and also reported on North Korea-Russia education cooperation talks.

30
00:05:20.160 --> 00:05:24.871
The rest of the news, quickly. Seoul has withdrawn embassy staff from Kyiv.

31
00:05:24.871 --> 00:05:39.006
The diplomatic pullback follows Ukraine's public disclosure of repatriated North Korean prisoners of war, and deepens an ongoing dispute between Seoul and Kyiv over how North Korea's role in the war is being handled publicly.

32
00:05:39.006 --> 00:05:51.255
Switzerland has dispatched a delegation to Pyongyang ahead of the opening of its first dedicated political office in North Korea, the first Swiss diplomatic presence there in more than six years.

33
00:05:51.255 --> 00:06:02.625
North Korea and Russia held talks between their education ministers on expanding bilateral exchanges, extending what state media describes as an institutional cooperation framework.

34
00:06:02.625 --> 00:06:12.991
A Seoul appeals court has again ordered a Japanese firm to pay compensation to forced labor victims, sustaining judicial pressure on Tokyo over wartime labor claims.

35
00:06:12.991 --> 00:06:24.424
On the economy: HD Korea Shipbuilding and Offshore Engineering won a 1.9 trillion won contract from HMM for eight container ships, adding to South Korea's shipbuilding order backlog.

36
00:06:24.424 --> 00:06:34.789
More than nine in ten bond market specialists surveyed expect the Bank of Korea to hold its policy rate at 2.50 percent at this week's monetary policy board meeting.

37
00:06:34.789 --> 00:06:52.189
Chinese memory chipmaker CXMT says it aims to break through ultra-fine process limits in DRAM production, reporting third-quarter revenue at the 23 trillion won level, a projected 600 percent year-on-year increase, posing a direct competitive challenge to Samsung and SK Hynix.

38
00:06:52.189 --> 00:07:05.256
President Lee Jae-myung called an IMF forecast of two consecutive years of rising potential growth in Korea a "dramatic rebound," noting a rise like that hasn't happened since the 1986 three lows boom period.

39
00:07:05.256 --> 00:07:16.940
At Tuesday's Cabinet meeting, Lee also pressed for accelerated housing supply measures and asked whether one Nuri rocket launch per year is sufficient given satellite and security needs.

40
00:07:16.940 --> 00:07:26.049
The inaugural chief of the Serious Crimes Investigation Agency, Kim Ji-yong, attended his first Cabinet meeting Tuesday, a first for that agency.

41
00:07:26.049 --> 00:07:32.645
Police also referred a series of financial institution hacking incidents to the agency for investigation.

42
00:07:32.645 --> 00:07:42.570
A South Korean nonprofit was fined 7 million won after an employee launched a balloon carrying personal data of 131 North Korean defectors toward North Korea.

43
00:07:43.170 --> 00:07:57.081
The Gallup Korea poll taken October 13 to 15 put President Lee's approval at 39 percent, up. The Democratic Party stood at 36 percent, also up. The People Power Party was at 27 percent, up as well.

44
00:07:57.081 --> 00:08:09.156
Independents fell to 29 percent. A piece in The Diplomat argues that restricted intelligence sharing, an unresolved investment dispute, and controversial remarks by a U.S.

45
00:08:09.156 --> 00:08:16.570
commander collectively point to structural strain in the U.S.-South Korea alliance, not routine friction.

46
00:08:17.770 --> 00:08:20.613
On Capitol Hill. Nothing on the Hill calendar today.

47
00:08:20.613 --> 00:08:33.244
But worth flagging for your read file: the Marine Corps commandant's parliamentary audit testimony on North Korean force changes along the border islands is the kind of assessment that typically surfaces in congressional briefings.

48
00:08:33.244 --> 00:08:37.290
Watch for it to make its way into alliance discussions in the weeks ahead.

49
00:08:38.490 --> 00:08:50.857
Beyond the region. The U.S. Army used South Korean-sourced ammunition in joint military drills with Philippine forces. The exercise reflects the growing role of South Korea's defense industry in U.S.

50
00:08:50.857 --> 00:08:59.060
military operations across the Indo-Pacific, separate from any direct South Korean military participation in the Philippines drills.

51
00:09:00.260 --> 00:09:16.224
Finally, the markets. The KOSPI closed at about 5,553, down nearly 4 percent. The won traded at about 1,327 to the dollar, up slightly. Brent crude was at about 111 dollars a barrel, up about 2 percent.

52
00:09:16.224 --> 00:09:25.550
That's the Korea Daily Brief for Tuesday, October 20. The full text, with a link to every source, is in today's email.
